 - Resolved in part ([#1831](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1831)): render targets that move in the game's 305 MB pool and change size with its dynamic resolution still get a new Metal texture each time. Their memory is now recycled ([Render targets that move with the dynamic resolution](../../../docs/reference/rendering.md#render-targets-that-move-with-the-dynamic-resolution-get-new-metal-memory-each-time)). Since 04419bc2 the resolution moves, and on a 16 GB MacBook Air M4 the game stuttered (p95 99 ms) with GPU allocation near 15 GB. On an M6, while the resolution moved in the village, the target site added 455-521 MB to the residency set per 300 frames without recycling and 27-37 MB with it once heaps were waiting, and frame p90 went from 33-50 ms to 17-18 ms. The live targets' steady total, about 2.1 GB in the village, cannot share the pool's memory as on the console: the game keeps live targets in the part of the full-size scene target its resolution leaves unused, which only NVN's layout allows (PR #1453). [A report of a GPU driver panic](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1299) lines up with this churn. The reporter's Mac has not been tested.
 - Current: the iPhone's first run (12 GB iPhone, guest files on, the phone's IPA at 34ada028) was ended for memory about 9.5 minutes in, at about 28 fps, at an area load: the log stops mid-frame with 5.96 GB of room reported at start, the jetsam limit's figure. On the Mac the same commit reached 6.36 GB of footprint stepping into the village, 4.4 GB of it Metal memory, 2.9 GB of that the 304 MB render-target pool's targets, nearly all of them unused for 20 s or more and drawn over by newer targets while their texture objects lived. They are now released ([Render targets drawn over stayed while their texture objects lived](../../../docs/reference/rendering.md#render-targets-drawn-over-stayed-while-their-texture-objects-lived)): on an M6, the village save and 2.5 minutes around Link's house, footprint 5.35–5.47 GB before, 3.70–3.80 GB after (one run each), 60 fps both, screenshots alike. Open: the iPhone run with it.
 - Measured (2026-11-14, [PR #2681](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2681)): held submissions ([PR #2645](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2645), which commits submissions that end while the GPU is busy later, together) do not raise memory at an area load on the iPhone 17. Four runs of one build loaded the owner's Temple of Time autosave from the title, alternating `DM_NVN_SUB_MERGE=0` and the default: the set's peak after a commit was 3,431 and 3,345 MB without merging, 3,425 and 3,403 MB with; the most wired 5,163 and 4,939 MB without, 5,191 and 5,092 MB with; the deferred-release queue at most 335 and 356 without, 263 and 256 with; no pressure event, thermal state 0-2. A load as heavy as the castle grounds' (a 2-s frame) was not measured.
+- A full model heap (`patches.py` `heap_retry`, `DM_HEAP_RETRY`, on by default): the model rebuild on the OptimizeChangeBank thread (main+0x4366e0) takes two CPU buffers and two blocks of GPU memory from small per-model heaps and checks none of them. HookLink's iPhone crashed 11 minutes in (`BufferInitialize: pool has no storage`, then `h_nvnMemoryPoolGetFlags` on a null pool, backtrace main+0x436fb4). Each failed allocation is now tried again in the heap's parents (at most 16), the GPU memory last in the game's default heap. The power line logs `full model heap` with the counts when they change. Hookshot found the same on PCs (NotRexed/Hookshot issue 1697). Open: not run in the game here, and the crash itself has not been reproduced on main.
 
